issue.body:
React version: `react@19.3.1` and `react-dom@19.3.1`, reproduced using the published packages in both development and production.

Environment: Node 24.22.1, macOS arm64.

## Steps To Reproduce

Install `react@19.3.1` and `react-dom@19.3.1`, then run this as an `.mjs` file:

```js
import React from 'react';
import {prerenderToNodeStream} from 'react-dom/static';
import {getEventListeners, once} from 'node:events';

const controller = new AbortController();
try {
  const {prelude} = await prerenderToNodeStream(
    React.createElement('div', null, 'hello'),
    {signal: controller.signal},
  );
  const before = getEventListeners(controller.signal, 'abort').length;
  const closed = once(prelude, 'close');
  prelude.destroy();
  await closed;
  await new Promise(resolve => setImmediate(resolve));
  await new Promise(resolve => setImmediate(resolve));

  console.log({
    before,
    after: getEventListeners(controller.signal, 'abort').length,
    destroyed: prelude.destroyed,
    closed: prelude.closed,
  });
} finally {
  controller.abort();
}
```

## The current behavior

The output is `{before: 1, after: 1, destroyed: true, closed: true}`. The unread stream has closed, but the caller's signal still holds its abort listener. That listener closes over the Fizz request and retains it while the signal remains alive.

I also reproduced this with `destroy(error)` and with a prelude returned by `resumeAndPrerenderToNodeStream`. The latter uses postponed state returned by the public prerender API, consumed and then serialised before resuming. Fully consuming a Node prelude or cancelling a Web prelude from `prerender` removes the listener as expected.

## The expected behavior

Destroying the Node prelude should notify Fizz that the consumer has cancelled, so the listener count becomes zero without also requiring the caller to abort its signal.

This is a follow-up to the lifetime cleanup in #37315 / #36763. The Node Readables in `ReactDOMFizzStaticNode` have a `read` hook but no destruction hook. Forwarding destruction to `stopFlowing` and `abort` makes the three regression cases pass while preserving the consumed-Node, cancelled-Web and stream-error controls.

The measurements above establish listener retention; they do not measure retained heap size.

React version: `react@19.3.1` and `react-dom@19.3.1`, reproduced using the published packages in both development and production.

Browser: Chromium 153.0.8010.12 on macOS.

## Steps To Reproduce

```jsx
import React from 'react';
import {flushSync} from 'react-dom';
import {createRoot} from 'react-dom/client';

const host = document.createElement('div');
document.body.appendChild(host);
const shadow = host.attachShadow({mode: 'open'});
const input = React.createRef();
const root = createRoot(shadow);

flushSync(() => {
  root.render(
    <input
      ref={input}
      defaultValue="hello"
      onSelect={event => {
        console.log('select', event.target.selectionStart, event.target.selectionEnd);
      }}
      onChange={event => console.log('change', event.target.value)}
    />,
  );
});

input.current.focus();
input.current.setSelectionRange(0, 0);
```

1. Press Shift+ArrowRight to select the first character.
2. Type `Z` to replace it.
3. Reload with `const shadow = host;` in place of the `attachShadow` line to run the ordinary-DOM control.

## The current behavior

Inside the shadow root, the selection becomes `[0, 1]` but `onSelect` is not called. Typing `Z` does call `onChange` with `"Zello"`. In the ordinary-DOM control, both callbacks work.

This also reproduces with `onSelectCapture`, a `<textarea>` and a closed shadow root. Automated keyboard checks in Chromium `153.0.8010.12` reproduced all four shadow-root cases; the two ordinary-DOM controls passed.

## The expected behavior

Keyboard selection should call `onSelect` for a focused React-rendered input or textarea inside a shadow root, as it does outside one.

`SelectEventPlugin.constructSelectEvent` compares its tracked input with `document.activeElement`. For an input inside a shadow root, the latter is the shadow host, so the plugin returns before dispatching the event. The input remains focused throughout; this does not require moving or reordering any nodes.

This is separate from #38082's focus restoration during reordering and #37695's missing event listeners on document roots.

A small fix using the field's own root for the focus check passes the regression cases, ordinary-root controls and an iframe document-isolation check. I also verified the change with a source build in Chromium.

