**Preserve scheduling for roots whose callbacks are in the rejected queue.**

Clearing this queue can also prevent a later successful `act` from applying
its own updates. With an already mounted counter:

```js
try {
  await act(async () => {
    setCount(1);
    await Promise.resolve();
    throw new Error('expected rejection');
  });
} catch {}

await act(async () => {
  setCount(2);
});
expect(container.textContent).toBe('2');
```

Both scopes are awaited, with the first rejection handled before the second
starts. On the PR base this renders `2`; on this head it remains `0`.

Before the rejection, the root-scheduling microtask has queued the render
callback and stored `fakeActCallbackNode` on the root. This assignment
discards the queue containing that callback, but leaves the root's
`callbackNode` and `callbackPriority` intact. The same-priority update in the
next scope then takes the reuse branch in
`ReactFiberRootScheduler.js:462-474`, so no callback is added to the new act
queue. This is separate from deciding whether to commit the update made in
the failed scope.

