Could this branch render the new fallback when an urgent update changes `fallbackChildren`? Reusing the dehydrated fragment with `null` here discards the new fallback props until the primary content resolves.

I reproduced this by hydrating a browser-only boundary whose client promise remains pending, then calling `root.render()` with a different fallback message. The screen keeps showing `Loading` instead of `Still loading`. This also affects loading/error guidance or theme changes while the request is pending.

The test below passes with the production source from base `d71b537` and fails at `c488f79`, in both development and production, using Node24.22.1 and Yarn1.22.22 on macOS arm64. The PR's three added tests pass. I ran these focused cases in `ReactDOMFizzServer-test.js`, using the same test harness for the base/head comparison.

```js
it('updates browser-only fallback props while client data is pending', async () => {
  const clientPromise = new Promise(() => {});

  function ClientOnly() {
    use(ReactDOM.browser());
    return <span>{use(clientPromise)}</span>;
  }

  function App({fallbackText}) {
    return (
      <Suspense fallback={<span>{fallbackText}</span>}>
        <ClientOnly />
      </Suspense>
    );
  }

  await act(() => {
    renderToPipeableStream(<App fallbackText="Loading" />, {
      onBrowserBailout() {},
    }).pipe(writable);
  });

  const root = ReactDOMClient.hydrateRoot(
    container,
    <App fallbackText="Loading" />,
  );
  await waitForAll([]);

  await clientAct(() => {
    root.render(<App fallbackText="Still loading" />);
  });
  expect(getVisibleChildren(container)).toEqual(<span>Still loading</span>);
});
```

I ran with `yarn test --dev --silent --no-watchman ReactDOMFizzServer-test --runInBand -t 'SSR fallback|browser-only fallback'`, then with `--prod`. For the base comparison I selected only `browser-only fallback`.

This is also the prop-update case called out in the discussion of #24236; the existing gated test titled “recreates the fallback if server errors and hydration suspends but client receives new props” describes the same expectation.
